 ### [Compositing Tree Rendering](5860-compositing-tree-rendering.md)
 A time-varying volume rendering strategy that avoids re-rendering unmodified data by retaining partial images of static subtrees and incrementally compositing them with newly rendered partial images from modified regions. It differs from standard incremental rendering by maintaining a persistent pointer-based octree structure (the compositing tree) that mirrors the data compression structure, allowing for efficient splicing of new branches and recomposition of ancestors, whereas methods like [Octree-based Entropy Rendering](13580-octree-based-entropy-rendering.md) focus on spatial resolution adaptation rather than temporal delta-compositing.
+
+### [Budgeted-GS Factoring LOD](9677-budgeted-gs-factoring-lod.md)
+A hierarchical level-of-detail method for real-time large-scale Gaussian Splatting that reduces rendering cost by aggregating primitives into equivalent planks based on a pixel-footprint budget. It solves the problem of rendering millions of Gaussians in real-time by dynamically selecting between raw primitives, fine equivalent planks, or coarse aggregate planks depending on their distance and scale relative to the camera. Unlike standard compression methods that operate in an over-budgeted regime with flat error curves, this method achieves a steeper error reduction by using a "floor law" to select the optimal budget, allowing for significant size reduction while maintaining visual fidelity.
 
